//~ compile_fail(backend)
//~ error[KORU030]: Phantom state mismatch

// NEGATIVE (must FAIL to compile): allocate memory and hand it straight back.
//
// THIS IS THE STRICT GATE, and it is the ruling that overruled 02c. That lift
// deliberately let `take` -> `free` compile, on three arguments: a heap
// allocation's use IS the reservation; the early-exit shape is ubiquitous; and
// ukalloc contains no assertion objecting to it, so there is nothing to lift.
//
// All three are true, and the ruling still went the other way, because a strict
// `free` plus a NAMED escape already CONTAINS the permissive design. Every
// program the permissive `free` accepts, this module accepts with one extra word
// in it — `alloc:untouched` — and that word buys something the permissive design
// cannot offer at any price:
//
//     grep -r "alloc:untouched"
//
// answers "where do we reserve memory we never write to", which is a question a
// unikernel with a 2 MB RAM floor genuinely asks. A permissive `free` cannot
// answer it, because the silent no-op and the deliberate reservation are spelled
// identically. The containment is asymmetric, so the strict reading wins.
//
// What the C does instead: nothing at all. `uk_malloc(a, 64)` followed by
// `uk_free(a, p)` is a well-formed, warning-free, sanitizer-clean C program that
// does no work. There is no `UK_ASSERT` for it and there could not be — the
// allocator has no idea whether you touched the memory. This is the one place in
// this lift where a phantom obligation is not transcribing an assertion Unikraft
// wrote, but keeping a guarantee Unikraft never had.
//
// See untouched_reservation.kz for the same program spelled honestly, which
// compiles clean and boots. What this file rejects is the SILENT no-op.
//
// EXPECTED: error[KORU030]: Phantom state mismatch: expected
//           'unikraft.alloc:live' but got 'unikraft.alloc:raw!'
//           for argument 'block'

// The `unikraft` namespace is declared here, not inherited: koruc's built-in
// alias probes the sibling koru-libs CHECKOUT, so in a worktree this file would
// otherwise compile against main's lifts instead of the ones beside it.
~std/compiler:paths {
    unikraft: {{ ENTRY }}/../..
}

~import unikraft/alloc
~import std/io

~unikraft/alloc:default
| ready heap |> unikraft/alloc:take(heap, bytes: 64)
    | block scratch |> unikraft/alloc:free(block: scratch)
        |> std/io:print.ln("should never get here")
    | exhausted e |> std/io:print.ln("take({{ e.bytes:d }}) failed: {{ e.reason:s }}")
| unavailable why |> std/io:print.ln("no allocator: {{ why:s }}")
